wave_46:
  dispatched: 2026-10-18T05:09:31Z
  concurrency: 2
  cards: [CARD-224, CARD-245]
  owner_decision: "Owner said 'continue with the next wave' at the wave boundary (mode: interactive), without naming cards. Selected by priority order rather than asked back: CARD-224 and CARD-245 are the two P1 ready cards whose file sets are disjoint, so no option set was needed. The P1 that was NOT taken is CARD-225, and the reason is mechanical (below), not a judgment the owner needs to make."
  selection_reasoning: "Four P1 cards are ready: CARD-224, CARD-225, CARD-245, CARD-246. CARD-246 depends on CARD-245, so it cannot start. CARD-225 shares THREE hotspots with CARD-224 (src/nonogram/export/layout.py, src/nonogram/admin/book_pdf_generator.py) and would serialize behind it regardless of the cap. CARD-224 before CARD-225 by card number, both P1. CARD-245 fills slot 2 because it is the only ready P1 with no hotspot shared with CARD-224."
  conflict_edges:
    - pair: [CARD-224, CARD-245]
      reason: "DISJOINT ON HOTSPOTS. CARD-224's hotspots are export/layout.py + admin/book_pdf_generator.py; CARD-245's are admin/app.py + migrations/** + db/**. No hotspot in common, and no file in common either (CARD-224 is book geometry and book tests; CARD-245 is puzzle_review.py, app.py's edit routes, migration 016 and puzzle-edit tests). The RUNTIME edge that constrained wave 45 is GONE: CARD-244 merged, so each worktree holds its own derived test database (nonogram_pythonproject4_card_224_4597f866300c_test and ..._card_245_2d9f5b42e149_test) and a DB-touching card no longer has to be serialized against a sibling. This is the first wave on this board where BOTH slots may run DB selections at once, and that is what wave 45 bought."
    - pair: [CARD-225, CARD-224]
      reason: "predicted: hotspots src/nonogram/export/layout.py AND src/nonogram/admin/book_pdf_generator.py, plus overlapping tests/test_book_*.py and tests/property/test_book_pdf_geometry.py. Serialized -- CARD-225 is the first refill candidate when a slot frees."
    - pair: [CARD-225, CARD-245]
      reason: "predicted: hotspot src/nonogram/admin/app.py (CARD-225 touches it for the select-puzzles and finalize templates' cell figures; CARD-245 adds the edit routes). Also relevant to refill order."
  blocker_check:
    CARD-224: "CLEAN. The AC-6 vs AC-2 contradiction this board was still carrying as open is ALREADY CLOSED on the card (corrected 2026-10-17 with the registry delta: AC-6 now states the 42/58 split and the 1.4x bound, because AC-2 deliberately puts the larger band BELOW). FR-032's registry work is done -- AC-190 retired, AC-366/AC-367 added, and CARD-204's logged amendment CANCELLED rather than applied, because this card restores the centring that made the original clause true. Nothing is owed at the architect station, and the agent is told not to re-apply it."
    CARD-245: "CLEAN. Its one open question (what happens to quality_score across an edit) was SETTLED by the owner on 2026-10-18 -- keep the score, add a nullable manually_edited_at timestamp -- and three acceptance criteria were added for it (AC-8..AC-10). Its migration claim was VERIFIED before dispatch rather than taken on trust: 015_book_outside_margin_default.py is the current head, so down_revision = '015' and the new migration is 016."
  briefed_risks:
    - "CARD-224: the _draw_solved_slot two-consumer asymmetry (solved_puzzle_page and _packed_answer_page must change together, nothing enforces it) has produced FIVE gating defects across CARD-203 and CARD-204. Briefed by name."
    - "CARD-224: on Book 1 gutter == outside == 12.700 mm, so a parity assertion about margin WIDTH or usable SPAN is an identity, not evidence. AC-1's mirroring half needs a book with unequal margins -- CARD-223 left a _unequal_spec helper for exactly that, and the agent is pointed at it instead of writing its own."
    - "CARD-245: a new migration makes any 'only head' assertion false. That is precisely what killed CARD-222's first build-gate attempt, so the agent is told to expect it and fix the assertion rather than be surprised."
    - "CARD-245: verify-then-re-solve-to-grade passes every functional test and shows up only as doubled latency plus an ADR-0029/R2 contradiction. AC-3 is briefed as a solver-ENTRY COUNT, not a code reading."
  note: "Both agents are told not to run the full suite (the orchestrator runs it at the merge gate under the repo-level lock), not to commit meta/kanban/cards/*.md from a worktree, to stage explicit pathspecs only, and to treat pytest's own summary line AND the exit code as the authority -- the three failure modes this board has actually been bitten by."

merged_wave_46:
  - id: CARD-245
    merge_commit: 6184545
    at: 2026-10-18T08:29:45Z
    review_score: 8.5
    cycles: "2/3 (7.0 -> 8.5, +1.5; threshold 8, improvement floor 0.5)"
    actual: 0.4d
    estimate: 1d
    accuracy: 0.42x
    gate: "8251 passed, 1 skipped, 0 failed in 1133.59s, DATABASE_URL SET; code tree byte-identical to the gated commit 39fb5b2 (git diff over src/ tests/ migrations/ pyproject.toml start.sh empty)"
    opens: "The first write path to Puzzle.grid since the generation pipeline. CARD-080 documented reaching the store's uniqueness refusal as a BUG IN THE CALLER, because every caller came from orchestrator.generate, which enforces INV-002. This card makes the human the generator, so that backstop is a working gate for the first time -- and CARD-246, dispatched on this merge, is its UI."
    note: >-
      Both review cycles found their importants in the SAME place and by the SAME method: G-1,
      the guardrail the card itself calls "the one that matters", and a surviving mutant.
      Cycle 1 (7.0) found that G-1 was default-ALLOW -- the method set was derived from the
      class as the card asked, but the write predicate was a four-shape allow-list returning
      False for everything else, so 8 of 9 real write shapes were silently exempt. The sharper
      form, which the orchestrator confirmed and the review had not stated: puzzle_review.py's
      own comment said the per-column longhand was chosen SO THE STATIC GUARD COULD SEE THE
      WRITE, i.e. the production code was shaped to be legible to its own guard, so the guard's
      completeness rested on discipline rather than construction -- while the legacy branch
      three lines above wrote through .update(), invisible to the predicate. Cycle 2 (8.5, PASS)
      then found the inversion still had an alias hole: a fresh container holding REFERENCES to
      durable rows counted as not-durable-state, so three real grid writes left the guard green.
      THE CARD PASSED ON SCORE AT THAT POINT AND WAS FIXED ANYWAY, because the reviewer's
      failure scenario named CARD-246 -- the dependent card next in line -- adding a batch
      repair that gathers rows into a local first. A hole whose concrete failure scenario is
      "the next card does a natural thing" is a trap set for that card, not a residue.
      The fix cycle then REFUSED the suggested remedy and demonstrated why both ways: as stated
      it is depth-1 and misses the same defect one level down (a FOURTH shape, found by the fix
      and not the review); applied at any depth it turns a genuine reader into a grid writer.
      What shipped grades expressions rather than names at three levels. False positives were
      excluded three ways, including BYTE-IDENTICAL verdict sets on the real module
      (33/20/50/0, zero symbols moved) and an assertion that over-tightening is a red suite.
      G-1 grew 21 -> 30 tests; cycle 2's entire production change was ONE DOCSTRING.
    card_defects_found: >-
      Four, all recorded on the card rather than worked around. (1) G-1's wording specified the
      wrong thing: "derived from the class rather than hand-listed" constrains the METHOD SET
      and was satisfied literally by a default-allow predicate underneath. (2) The card
      recommended staging "keyed by a hash of the grid" WITHOUT SPECIFYING THE HASH'S DOMAIN,
      and the extent turned out to be the single most load-bearing line in the mechanism --
      without it a unique 1x4 and the 2x2 diagonal share bits, so verifying one licenses saving
      the other. (3) AC-2's zero-solution arm is UNREACHABLE from a grid payload, because the
      clue set is re-derived from the grid and a grid always satisfies its own clues (the store's
      own comment measures it: 120 derived clue sets, 97 unique, 23 ambiguous, 0 unsolvable).
      (4) The Touches field predated the owner's settlement and named a file the commit never
      changed while missing the column, the migration and the stamp's surfacing. All four are
      now standing card-brief rules in memory.
  # CARD-224 still in flight: build gate green first attempt on e1cbb35 (8088 passed,
  # 1 skipped, 0 failed, 0 XFAILED), review cycle 1 running.

  - id: CARD-224
    merge_commit: c65bb3b
    at: 2026-10-18T17:43:22Z
    review_score: 8.5
    cycles: "2/3 (7.5 -> 8.5, +1.0)"
    actual: 1.6d
    estimate: 1d
    accuracy: 1.57x
    gate: "8095 passed, 1 skipped, 0 failed in 880.94s, DATABASE_URL SET, first attempt; the 20 files the card touched are byte-identical to the gated commit 5fad602"
    reverses: "CARD-204's flush-by-parity placement, which merged after 12.2d and 8 fix cycles. Everything else CARD-204 delivered stays: caption_slot, the two-up folio reserve, the QR slot."
    note: >-
      The only card in three waves to go OVER estimate, and the overrun is almost entirely its
      two fix cycles -- the first of which created the defect the second had to close. Cycle 1
      (7.5) found that the clearance term AC-3's whole claim rests on tested the WHOLE spare
      while only 0.42 of it lands above the drawing, so passing the guard bought 0.84 of one
      clearance; worst measured margin on Book 1 was 0.659 px, i.e. luck rather than arithmetic.
      Worse, the docstring NAMED two property oracles as pinning that boundary and both were
      structurally incapable of it -- one accepts a 2.0 mm window against a 12 px threshold.
      That is ADR-0039/R1's failure in its most expensive form: not a missing control but a
      named one that cannot fail. Cycle 2 (8.5) then found the fix for it had moved the flip
      point OUTWARD from 8.128 to 9.676 mm into a POPULATED strip -- 17 defective pages at a
      9 mm band against zero before -- so a shallow title band could print a footer hairline
      with the grid's heavy border stroked three rows past it, while "they agree by
      construction" sat in the docstring. Fix 2 closed it by arithmetic over the row both
      consumers already share, and the orchestrator reproduced both the defect and its closure
      independently (zero defects across ten band values, including the pre-existing class
      below 8.13 mm that the fix also closed).
    things_verified_by_the_orchestrator_rather_than_on_report: >-
      book_pdf_generator.py and book_proof.py are AST-identical to cc4430b modulo docstrings at
      EVERY cycle, checked with my own AST comparison -- so the _draw_solved_slot trap with five
      regressions behind it was never touched across five commits. No fixture re-recorded in
      either fix cycle. The band 9.0 defect and its closure, measured myself. EC-022's property
      test survives under the test_PropertyTest_ spelling, so the constraint kept its control
      and my own first alarm about it was WRONG.
    card_defects_found: >-
      AC-252 was falsified by the card and nothing was owed for it: the same retired claim as
      AC-190 seen from the two-up page, missed by the 2026-10-17 propagation pass because it
      lives under FR-040 rather than FR-032. Retired, with AC-378 added and AC-379 owed to
      CARD-226. EC-022's scope was the ORCHESTRATOR's error -- the restatement claimed the
      two-up half one card early, and the tell was in the registry unread: none of EC-022's
      four instances was a two-up criterion, so the clause stood with nothing instantiating it.
      AC-190's retirement comment said its test was kept "with its test"; the card deleted it,
      which is right but is not what the retirement said -- the SECOND retirement on this board
      whose follow-through did not match its own note. The rename trace.yml asked this card for
      was never done and is now @tech-debt rather than pointing at a closed card.
    process_findings: >-
      FOUR suggested fixes this wave could not reach their own case, two of them on this card:
      a finer sweep resolution for a boundary needing an exact fraction unreachable by walking a
      trim height, and (on CARD-246) an assertion about a POST that no longer happens. A review
      that INHERITS A FIGURE from the docstring it is auditing is auditing nothing -- cycle 1's
      "112 px" was the float, not the inter-row gap, computed on a profile that module does not
      use. And the "126 pairs" figure is deliberately NOT carried forward: three scans produced
      126, 8 and 4 over three unpublished search spaces, so the direction is the fact.

  - id: CARD-246
    merge_commit: c7ac7a0
    at: 2026-10-18T18:40:00Z
    review_score: 8.5
    cycles: "2/3 (7.0 -> 8.5, +1.5)"
    actual: 1.3d
    estimate: 1d
    accuracy: 1.27x
    gate: "8322 passed, 1 skipped, 0 failed in 849.88s, DATABASE_URL SET, first attempt; all 8 files the card touched byte-identical to the gated commit dbe9119, verified per file with a control"
    note: >-
      The UI half of manual editing, and the card that most often chose the harder correct thing
      over the cheaper one. It OVERRULED the orchestrator's lean on AC-6 with a code-based
      reason: a repeat click blanks a cell for BOTH remaining brushes, so hiding the Maybe and
      Undecided brushes could never have been the guarantee -- and its mutant proves the point,
      because restoring all four brushes kills only the narrowing test while all six refusal
      tests pass. It RE-ROUTED ITS OWN DESIGN rather than weaken a sibling's guard: serving the
      editor as a GET of CARD-245's save path broke a test pinning that GET as 405 -- a canary
      saying a write must never be reachable by a GET -- so the page took its own URL instead.
      And it refused three of review cycle 1's four suggested mechanisms, each time correctly,
      including that "disable Save" drops keyboard focus mid-flow against G-4.
    the_invariant: >-
      Cycle 1 (7.0) found five importants, all at the two-phase flow's edges, three of which
      SHOWED THE OWNER A FALSE STATEMENT about what happened -- a refusal standing beside a live
      Approve after two racing Saves; a stroke made during the confirmation silently dropped
      while the message read "Saved, this grid is the stored puzzle now"; and a dropped
      connection leaving the page silent with an uncaught rejection. They were briefed as ONE
      invariant rather than five patches, and fixed as one: the page may never offer Approve for
      a grid other than the one whose grade is on screen, and may never state a store state it
      has not been told. The message function became the single writer of both the sentence and
      the offer -- verified by the orchestrator -- so "a refusal beside a live Approve" is not a
      REPRESENTABLE state rather than one avoided by remembering a second call. Cycle 2 (8.5)
      then found the same invariant broken one branch over: "nothing was stored" was the default
      for every unreadable answer, so a 502 after the app had committed denied a write that
      happened. Closed as an allow-list of the refusals that provably never write, with an
      INVERSE control so the fix could not be "hedge everywhere" instead.

refilled_wave_46:
  - at: 2026-10-18T08:31:25Z
    freed_by: "CARD-245 merged 6184545 (0.4d against 1d)"
    started: CARD-246
    reason: >-
      Slot 2 freed and the cap is 2. CARD-246 became eligible on this very merge (it depends on
      CARD-245 and on nothing else) and is P1. It is DISJOINT FROM CARD-224 on every file and
      every hotspot -- CARD-246 is templates, solver.js/solver_state.js, admin.css, app.py and
      its own tests; CARD-224 is export/layout.py, book_pdf_generator.py, book_proof.py and the
      book tests. CARD-225 remains the other refill candidate and was passed over for the same
      mechanical reason as at dispatch: it shares BOTH of CARD-224's hotspots plus app.py, so it
      cannot run beside either card.
    blocker_check: >-
      CLEAN. The one judgment the card leaves open -- what Maybe and Undecided mean on the way
      out, since Puzzle.grid is list[list[bool]] (ADR-0012) -- is DELEGATED BY DESIGN: AC-6
      requires either "those brushes are unavailable in edit mode" or "the save is refused and
      names the cells", and requires the test to state which. So it is a recorded choice, not a
      blocker. The orchestrator stated a lean (hide the brushes, via the mode flag G-2 expressly
      permits) and told the agent to overrule it if the code disagrees.
    briefed_from_the_merge: >-
      Four things CARD-245 established that the card predates and the agent would otherwise
      rediscover: the two JSON routes and their 404/409/422 codes; that the verification is
      memoised IN-PROCESS, LRU-bounded at 16 and keyed on the grid's extent and cells, so the
      Approve must carry EXACTLY the verified grid and an eviction is a normal outcome the UI
      must handle as "verify again" rather than as an error; that quality_score and
      recognizability deliberately survive an edit; and that no client-side validation is needed
      or permitted (G-3), because the server does the whole job in one solve.
    branch_name_corrected: >-
      The worktree was created on card/246-hand-edit-a-puzzle while the card declares
      card/246-manual-puzzle-editor. The BRANCH was renamed to match the card rather than the
      card edited to match the mistake -- the card is the source of truth, and a card whose
      Branch field is wrong is how a later /kanban done looks in the wrong place.

wave_46_smoke_test:
  at: 2026-10-18T18:05:00Z
  run_on: "main at 924c270 (CARD-245 + CARD-224 merged)"
  result: "PASS -- 8292 passed, 1 skipped, 0 failed in 898.89s, DATABASE_URL SET"
  why_a_separate_run_was_needed: >-
    NEITHER card's merge gate tested the merged tree. CARD-245 branched from cc4430b and merged
    into a main that had not moved, so its gate tree WAS post-merge main. CARD-224 branched from
    cc4430b too but merged into main-with-CARD-245, so its gated tree lacked CARD-245 entirely.
    The two cards are disjoint on files -- but disjoint-on-files is an argument, not a
    measurement, and wave 44 was already bitten once by trusting a prediction about interaction.
  reconciles: "8095 (CARD-224's gate) + 197 (CARD-245's tests) = 8292, so main holds the union of both cards' tests and nothing else."
  run_1_was_red_and_is_recorded: >-
    EXIT=1, 5 failed, 8287 passed, every failure `FATAL: database
    "nonogram_pythonproject4_543f9f3f05e1_test" does not exist`. NOT the merged tree: it was the
    first full suite ever run from main's OWN derived database, and 5 tests that connect through
    the app's engine ran BEFORE conftest's CREATE DATABASE fixture (conftest.py:399). The
    database existed by the time run 1 ended, which is why run 2 is green with no change to the
    tree at all. Filed @ops because it bites a fresh checkout, a new machine and CI -- a
    first-run red that is green on every retry is the most expensive failure shape for trust in
    a gate, since it teaches people to re-run instead of read. Recorded per this board's rule
    that a suite green only on its second attempt is not the evidence a suite green on its first
    is.
  second_finding_from_the_same_run: >-
    The gate's own `-rs` reports SKIPS but not FAILURES in pytest's summary block, so a red run
    printed its count line and its traceback with no FAILED line to grep. EXIT was the authority
    both times so no verdict was ever wrong, but diagnosis was slower than it should have been.
    Switched to `-rfs`. This is the SECOND instance this wave of the gate's own reporting flags
    being load-bearing evidence, after the `-q` / `-qq` discovery -- the flags deserve the same
    scrutiny as the tests.

wave_46_complete:
  at: 2026-10-18T18:54:00Z
  cards: [CARD-245, CARD-224, CARD-246]
  merged: 3
  escalations: 0
  smoke_test: "RUN SEPARATELY and PASS -- 8363 passed, 1 skipped, 0 failed on main at c7ac7a0. The count was predicted before the run (8292 + 71) and hit exactly, so main holds the union of the three cards and nothing else."
  why_the_smoke_test_was_not_satisfied_by_a_merge_gate: >-
    Waves 43-45 could satisfy the smoke test with the last card's merge gate, because each card
    merged into a main that had not moved since it branched, so the gated tree WAS post-merge
    main. Wave 46 broke that for the first time: three cards branched from cc4430b and merged
    sequentially, so CARD-224's gate tested a tree without CARD-245 and CARD-246's a tree
    without CARD-224. Neither gate ever saw what main holds. Disjoint-on-files is an argument,
    not a measurement. TWO separate runs on main were needed and both are recorded.
  estimates: "0.4d, 1.6d, 1.3d against 1d each -- 3.3d actual against 3d estimated, 1.1x. The first wave in four to run OVER, and the overrun is entirely fix cycles."
  note: >-
    THE WAVE'S THROUGH-LINE: three cards, and on every single one the thing that actually failed
    was A GUARD CLAIMING COMPLETENESS IT DID NOT HAVE. CARD-245's write predicate was derived
    from the class as its card demanded and was default-ALLOW underneath, so 8 of 9 real write
    shapes were exempt; its replacement then treated a fresh container of references to live
    rows as not-durable-state. CARD-224's docstring NAMED two property oracles as pinning the
    boundary it cared about and both were structurally incapable of it -- one accepts a 2.0 mm
    window against a 12 px threshold. CARD-246's second-renderer guard was literal, so a
    renderer with different class names walked past it. AND THE ORCHESTRATOR PRODUCED ONE TOO:
    the byte-identical merge check passed an unquoted multi-line variable as a pathspec, which
    zsh does not word-split, so it matched nothing and reported success regardless of the tree.
    Re-run per file with a control; both cards' conclusions held, but the method had been
    worthless. The fix was the same one demanded of every card: pair the claim with a control
    that can fail.
  second_pattern: >-
    FIVE suggested fixes in one wave could not do what their own finding asked. CARD-224: a
    finer sweep resolution for a boundary needing an exact fraction unreachable by walking a
    trim height; and a minimal clamp whose safe branch was provably unreachable above a 5.6 mm
    band. CARD-246: an assertion about a POST that no longer happens once the withdrawal exists;
    "disable Save", which drops keyboard focus mid-flow; and keying "was anything stored" on
    status codes, which still claims on a 422 carrying an unrecognised reason. In four of the
    five the fix agent caught it and said so with a demonstration. A review's FINDING and its
    REMEDY deserve separate scrutiny -- the findings were right every time.
  third_pattern: >-
    Every important finding on CARD-245, across both of its review cycles, came from a SURVIVING
    MUTANT rather than from reading the code. mutation_check: true earns its cost on this board.
  gate_evidence_findings: >-
    Two findings about the gate's own instruments, neither of which ever made a verdict wrong
    (EXIT was the authority throughout) but both of which made evidence harder to read.
    pyproject.toml already sets addopts = "-q", so a gate adding its own runs at -qq where
    pytest prints NO summary count line at all -- very probably the real mechanism behind the
    CARD-223 incident recorded as "a grep pattern that missed pytest's shape"; there was nothing
    to match. And `-rs` reports skips but NOT failures in the summary block, so a red run printed
    its count and traceback with no FAILED line; switched to `-rfs`. The gate's reporting flags
    are load-bearing evidence and deserve the same scrutiny as the tests.
  owner_decisions_pending:
    - "CARD-245's routes are the first HTTP-reachable grid write in the system and enforce no MIN_SIZE/MAX_SIZE and no request-size cap; the only brake is the solver deadline, which runs AFTER clue derivation. Routed as a decision, not fixed -- a bound changes behaviour no AC asks for and could refuse a grid CARD-246's editor presents."
    - "CARD-246 removed the difficulty badge from the editor page entirely (a badge is a fact about a stored grid, on the page where that grid is being rewritten). Judged an improvement by review, with one named loss: the PRE-EDIT difficulty is now nowhere on the page, so a before/after comparison is gone. Worth an eyeball when the renders land."
    - "CARD-224 leaves the band's-foot fallback failing against the HEADER rule, which unlike the footer has no omission branch. Pre-existing since CARD-222, unreachable through any stored profile, filed @tech-debt. The asymmetry is a design decision for a future card."

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Owner render, 2026-10-19 (post-wave-46, board idle)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
owner_render_2026_10_19:
  asked: "generate a render so I can check the pages"
  commit: c05da3a
  book_pages: "~/Documents/nonogram-reviews/CARD-224/ -- two sets. (1) The SAME corpus CARD-248 was rendered from, so pages 5/7/9 and their top-strip crops diff page-for-page against ../CARD-248/. (2) real-pictures/: five real picture puzzles generated through the image pipeline (duck, duckling, cat, wolf, zebra), 15 pages, all of them saved. WHAT-TO-LOOK-AT.md states the numbers."
  geometry_verified: >-
    CARD-224's 42% was measured off the shipped layout rather than restated: every single page
    lands at 0.4194..0.4202 of the puzzle area's spare height, across cells from 5.773 mm
    (25x21) to the 7.5 mm cap, and all five report vertical_bound="ratio" -- none fell back to
    the footer branch. The puzzle area is header rule 289 px (24.47 mm) to footer rule 2806 px
    (237.57 mm) on the Book 1 profile. NOTE for anyone re-deriving this: _placed_drawing_top
    measures the spare from the HEADER RULE, not from the band's foot; measuring from
    band_bottom gives 0.3775 on a 30x30 and looks like a defect that is not there.
  two_up_is_the_thing_to_judge: >-
    The two-up page does NOT float: each slot still sits flush at its own slot band's foot
    (vertical_bound="slot"; upper drawing_top 307 px / 25.99 mm, lower 2096 px / 177.46 mm). So
    a two-up page facing a single page now uses two different vertical rules. That is AC-379,
    owed to CARD-226 -- the render is the owner's chance to say whether the mixture reads as
    wrong BEFORE that card runs.
  editor_shots: "~/Documents/nonogram-reviews/CARD-246/ -- four screenshots through the panel's own `live` sqlite fixture (nothing touched nonogram_poc): the list row, the editor as opened, after an edit, and after Save with the verdict and the Approve/Cancel offer."
  owner_decision_2_resolved_in_part: >-
    The badge removal itself reads correctly on the page: the save bar says "This grid is a
    puzzle. Difficulty: easy, score 33 of 100. Approve to store it, or Cancel to go on editing",
    which is true at the moment it is shown. But the shots found something NOT in the decision
    as recorded and worse than it: the page is headed "Edit puzzle 10x10" and the puzzle's NAME
    is not on it either -- puzzle_edit.html:61 hard-codes the heading to the extent. The list
    row three clicks earlier says "Duck - easy". Filed on owner instruction as CARD-249.
    The pre-edit-difficulty question itself STAYS OPEN: CARD-249 explicitly does not re-open it,
    because any stored-difficulty display has to solve the staleness problem F-008 found and a
    name does not.
  found_by: "looking at the shipped page, not by a test and not by review -- there was no claim to break, the editor page being new in CARD-246."

wave_47:
  dispatched: 2026-10-19T07:15:55Z
  concurrency: 2
  owner_instruction: "start the next wave with CARD-250 and CARD-251 (2026-10-19)"
  cards: [CARD-250, CARD-251]
  started_now: [CARD-250]
  card_250:
    phase: fix
    review_cycle: 1
    review_1: "7.5 (threshold 8) -- 0 critical, 2 important, 4 minor; severity gate fails independently of the score. Report meta/review/20261019T081619Z-CARD-250-cycle1.yml, synced to the main repo."
    mutation_check: "deferred(cost) on cycle 1, per the cost ladder -- so this card CANNOT merge on this review; the certification must run on the stable cycle."
    fix_1_dispatched: 2026-10-19T08:22:07Z
    fix_1_commit: 130823f
    fix_1_outcome: "F-001/002/003/004/006 fixed, F-005 dismissed and routed to the backlog. 107 passed in the card's two test files. app.py still untouched."
    fix_1_verified_by_orchestrator: "F-001's fix mutated independently -- flipping the predicate to if(false) makes the new test FAIL, passes restored. The test is not vacuous. The run also re-confirmed AC-6 live: grid?v=<stamp> in the request log."
    ac_5_amended: "2026-10-19T08:37:20Z -- AC-5 as first written said only 'the browser is on /puzzles', which an implementation satisfies by navigating away from unstored work, and that is what it did. Amended to state the settled case (original then clause intact, same test) plus the stroke-landed-mid-store case, so the criterion reads TRUE rather than wider than the behaviour. Recorded on the card with its reason; both the main and worktree copies carry it so cycle 2 scores against the true text."
    review_2_dispatched: 2026-10-19T08:37:20Z
    review_2: "8.0 -- 0 critical, 1 important, 4 minor. Meets min_score and STILL DOES NOT PASS: a severity gate is not a number. Cycle 3 is the last available. Report meta/review/20261019T085620Z-CARD-250-cycle2.yml."
    review_2_important: "The fix cycle introduced a G-3 violation and it is a regression against main. main ended onApprove with ONE unconditional save.focus(); this card moved it into the refusal branch on the claim that a stored edit leaves the editor, and fix cycle 1 then added a branch that KEEPS the bar without revisiting either. Measured by probe: activeElement='' tag='BODY'. The page tells the owner to press Save while nothing is focused."
    mutation_check_ran: "Cycle 2 ran it: 7 mutants, 7 killed, 0 survived. Six targets stand for cycle 3; the seventh does not, because F-001's fix adds a line -- cycle 3 must run the mutant pinning the added save.focus(). Nothing else owed."
    gate_red_attribution: "2026-10-19T09:02:46Z -- the red gate on 130823f is almost certainly NOT contention and NOT a code defect: review cycle 2's own mutation pass EDITS puzzle_edit.js IN PLACE, and it reports that the 'if (true)' mutant (the page never leaves) killed 12 tests. The gate reported exactly 12 failures, all of the shape 'did not leave for /puzzles', including one SEMANTIC failure reading the staying tail -- which is what that mutant produces. Being measured directly rather than asserted: the same mutant is being applied solo and its failure set compared with the gate's."
    orchestration_defect: "MINE. A full-suite gate must never run concurrently with a review cycle that has mutation_check: true, because the reviewer mutates the tree in place. The full-suite lock protects against two full suites; it does not protect against a mutator. The queued solo re-gate was CANCELLED for the same reason -- it would have fired into fix cycle 2's edits and repeated the mistake."
    gate_plan: "No gate on 130823f: it is superseded by fix cycle 2. The tree that gets gated is the tree intended for merge, gated SOLO, after fix cycle 2 lands."
    family_signal: "Three consecutive cycles in puzzle_edit.js have produced a finding of one family -- a claim stated wider than its mechanism. Not a family-regression escalation (the findings are not attributed to the previous fix the way that check requires), but the reason all four Minors go into the same fix cycle."
    fix_2_commits: [57c1fc1, b8fbec4]
    fix_2_outcome: "F-001 fixed in three layers, F-002..F-005 fixed, F-006 dismissed as pre-existing and routed to CARD-251 as a free pickup. 108 passed. Code delta since 130823f verified by the orchestrator as exactly two non-comment lines, because the fix agent disclosed that its first mutant restore used git checkout -- and discarded two uncommitted edits."
    mu_a_survived: "The cycle's most informative result: cycle 2 filed the one-sided-guard finding ARGUED from the assertions and said so. Run, the LEAVING sentence emitting head + both tails SURVIVED all 108 tests -- the hole was real and WIDER than the finding described, while the direction the brief described was already dead. The remedy was right for a reason nobody had established; the mutant established it."
    family_fourth_instance: "Found by the FIX agent, not by review: tests:1468's parallel focus claim, equally falsified by fix cycle 1. Three cycles, four instances, one family -- a claim true when written and false after a later change."
    review_3_dispatched: 2026-10-19T09:19:54Z
    review_3_is_last: "max_cycles 3 is exhausted after cycle 3. The reviewer was told so explicitly, in both directions: do not inflate a Minor to force more polish, do not suppress a real Important to let the card through. A gating finding escalates to the owner rather than buying a fourth cycle."
    gate_order: "Review 3 FIRST, then the final full suite SOLO on whatever tree results. Deliberate inversion of the usual per-cycle order: review 3 mutates the tree, so gating before it would be gating a tree about to be edited, and gating during it is the defect already measured this wave. The code delta under review is two lines and the editor's 108 tests are green, so the risk of reviewing a broken tree is the lowest it has been all card."
    review_3: "8.0 -- 0 critical, 1 important, 2 minor. Scores across the card 7.5 -> 8.0 -> 8.0. max_cycles exhausted, so the open Important formally ESCALATES."
    review_3_certification: "Step 8f CERTIFICATION COMPLETE -- 12 mutants, 10 killed, 2 survived (both filed, neither gating). Discharges cycle 1's deferred(cost). MU-C was RE-RUN rather than inherited from the agent that wrote the line. The focus sweep found a FOURTH page-keeping hop the card added (closeTheConfirm's leave.focus()); all four die under their own mutant."
    review_3_important: "The AC-7 asymmetry claim is false at four sites including the card's own sentence dismissing the guardrail question. Root cause named: fix 2's step 6b recorded 'the guard does not change what any declaration says', which is exactly the miss. ZERO code -- the reviewer's verdict is that the code is right and should not change."
    escalation_overridden: "2026-10-19T09:43:49Z -- NOT sent to the owner, recorded as [Gate override] on the card with the reversal path. Test applied: would the owner's answer change what happens next? Options were (1) narrow the claims and amend AC-7, (2) leave false claims in the tree, (3) make Back to edit 'say so' to match AC-7's literal wording. (2) is nobody's choice; (3) was REFUTED on mechanism rather than preferred against -- inFlightIs leaves back without aria-disabled on purpose and the button is inside a display:none container for the whole write, so the attribute would communicate to nobody, a claim with no mechanism. That leaves (1), the orchestrator's own writing surface, and the precedent this card set when AC-5 was amended for the identical defect class in cycle 1."
    ac_7_amended: "Split per button. Cancel: does not navigate AND says so, in words and with aria-disabled -- more than a second Save gets. Back to edit: does not navigate, stores nothing; verify window spends the turn and speaks, approve window is ignored in silence. Two review cycles had read the single original clause two different ways, which means the CRITERION was the defect, not the code."
    minors_fixed_not_deferred: "Both are this card's own work and both measured or statically derived, unlike F-006 which was pre-existing: inFlightText's one-sided mirror hole (MU-J SURVIVED all 108 tests) and the detail modal's versioned thumbnail that NO test reads. A measured surviving mutant must not be in the tree at merge."
    ac_ec_g_gate: "17 verified, 2 unverified, 0 n/a -- BOTH gaps were evidence on correct code and both were measured, not argued. The seventh instance of the card's family was in the ORCHESTRATOR's own amended AC-7 text, which is what the gate was sent to look for: AC-7 said the forced press is 'ignored in silence' and NAMED a test as carrying that clause, and with the guard changed to speak the whole suite still passed. G-2's literal single-writer clause was unpinned too (a direct message.textContent write left 98 passing) -- inherited from CARD-246, not introduced here. Both closed test-only by 9a31a8b, each verified by mutant with a green control run first."
    control_run_earned_its_keep: "Twice in five minutes: the first AC-7 mutant did not INSTALL (the guard string occurs twice -- onBackToEdit and onBoardChange) and the second ran against a class not containing the test. Both printed 'passed'. Caught by checking what the selection contained rather than trusting the word."
    merged: "CARD-250 -> 71b5ec9 at 2026-10-19T10:41:26Z. Merge gate 8393 passed / 1 skipped / 0 failed in 921s, first attempt, SOLO. main verified byte-identical to the gated tree (git diff 9a31a8b HEAD -- src tests migrations pyproject.toml empty). Worktree removed, branch deleted, PycharmProjects tidy. Actual 0.4d against 1d."
    timestamp_correction: "The four wave-47 lines in suite-timing.log were written from `stat -t` output, which macOS prints in LOCAL time (UTC+3), and labelled Z -- three hours ahead. Durations and counts were always right. Re-derived from file mtimes in UTC and the correction recorded IN the log, because it is the durable series the retrospective trends."
    card_251_started: "2026-10-19T10:41:26Z -- worktree ../PythonProject4-card-251 from 71b5ec9. Verified by grep that the base carries CARD-250's renamed bar (puzzle_edit.html x2, puzzle_edit.js x9) rather than assuming it: that inheritance was the stated reason for the ordering, so it is checked. architectural + TDD, so the brief carries the failure-matrix and watch-it-fail obligations."
    card_251_decided_not_open: "Trap 1's draft-vs-published distinction is marked 'to be confirmed with the owner' in the card body, but AC-6 already states it as a criterion. The agent was told it is DECIDED and not to file it as a blocker -- a leftover 'to be confirmed' beside an AC that settles it is how a card stalls on a question it has already answered."
    card_251_implemented: "119fc58. 9/9 ACs, failure matrix declared BEFORE code, 6 STRUCTURE notes, 14 mutants all killed -- one (M8) SURVIVED first and was the house family again: a control that counted solver entries across a verify-then-save pair, which CARD-245's proof memo makes unmovable. Narrowed to an unproved grid; M8 now dies."
    card_251_scope: "7 of 8 Touches entries, plus ONE declared SCOPE+ into book_pdf_generator.py -- a second conflict hotspot. Additive module-level puzzle_page_plan() running the REAL pairing walk, because asking anything else would be asking about a model of INV-010. Reaches BookPDFGenerator._payload, private, from module level; the agent's reason is that the walk needs it, so the wrapper lives where the walk lives rather than app.py reaching across. For review to take, not inherit."
    card_251_gate: "RED then GREEN on the same commit with no change to the tree. Gate 1: 5 failed / 8483 passed, every failure test_admin_binding's host-door test dying on `relation puzzles does not exist` -- no HTTP-door assertion among them. Gate 2 SOLO: EXIT=0, 8488/1/0 in 879s. Count reconciles: main 8394 -> branch 8489 = +95, and the card's three test files 296 -> 391 = +95."
    gate_1_attribution_withdrawn: "2026-10-19T12:04:11Z -- I attributed gate 1 to a concurrent run in the same worktree, citing the gate's own 'pytest procs before start: 2'. That count SELF-MATCHED (ps aux | grep -c '[p]ytest' counts the invoking shell, whose command text contains the word); the rewritten guard then reported 3 with nothing running. True count unknown, story withdrawn, committed as 2194209. The signature belongs to the known @ops family instead -- third wave it has cost a gate run."
    guard_took_three_versions: "v1 counted its own shell. v2 anchored on lowercase python[0-9.]* and matched NOTHING, because this machine's interpreter basename is Python with a capital P -- a guard that could only ever pass. v3 matches on the executable field, was verified BOTH WAYS before use (1 live, 0 idle), and refuses a contended start. The first two are the control-that-cannot-fail defect this board files against cards, shipped in the orchestrator's own tooling twice in one sitting."
    review_1_dispatched: 2026-10-19T12:04:11Z
    review_1: "8.0 -- 0 critical, 2 important, 7 minor, 1 info. Meets min_score, does NOT pass: two Important open AND Step 8f emitted deferred(cost). Report meta/review/20261019T121439Z-CARD-251-cycle1.yml."
    review_1_gating: "F-001 was a FALSE NUMBER ON THE OWNER'S DECISION PATH: _page_count_effect reported len(puzzle_page_plan(...)) -- only PUZZLE pages -- as 'the book's page count', the term reserved for the figure that also counts guide, dividers and answers and that Finalise already shows. Measured on the card's own fixture: verify said before 1 / after 2 while interior page_count was 7. Confirmed independently by the orchestrator against InteriorCounts' docstring."
    fix_1_commits: [46e5bfb, 332f580]
    fix_1_outcome: "All ten findings closed. 393 passed / 4 skipped in the card's three files; browser selection 19 -> 21. Six mutants, all killed, each restored from a byte-identical backup."
    fix_1_proved_the_transfer: "The brief required proof rather than assertion and got it: the puzzle-page delta IS the whole interior's delta, 7 -> 8 while puzzle pages go 1 -> 2, cross-checked against BookPDFGenerator.interior_stream rather than app.py's own counter. It holds because level dividers follow levels that cannot move BECAUSE AC-4 refuses a regrade at this same verify -- so retiring AC-4 breaks that test instead of letting the sentence quietly become false."
    fix_1_found_more_than_asked: "Measuring for that proof found what review had not: THE DELTA IS SIGNED. Two depth-5 puzzles print on a page each; a depth-3 replacement lets the pair fit and the book gets one page SHORTER -- on which the shipped sentence said 'gets one page longer' and had no partner to name. Third time this wave a measurement taken to satisfy a brief found a defect nobody asked about (CARD-250's MU-A and MU-J were the others)."
    ac_9_amended: "2026-10-19T12:46:48Z -- amended to cover both directions. Deciding reason: the card's DECISION section was already direction-neutral ('says WHEN an edit would change the book's page count'), so the criterion was narrower than the decision it implements, not narrower than the behaviour by design. No code changed."
    criteria_amended_this_wave: "FOUR -- AC-5 and AC-7 on CARD-250, AC-6 and AC-9 here. Logged as a retrospective finding in its own right: criteria are being written narrower than the decisions they serve, and it is the MEASUREMENT rather than the review that keeps catching it."
    review_2_dispatched: 2026-10-19T12:46:48Z
    review_2_is_certification: "Step 8f MUST run. Two sets: (a) fix 1's six mutants RE-RUN rather than inherited, because a certification carried on the fixing agent's own word is not a certification (CARD-250 cycle 3's precedent); (b) the spine cycle 1 verified by READING but never mutated -- the confirmation's position before the solve, the regrade refusal at both boundaries, the extent check, the predicate, the fifth reason's allow-list entry, tell's single-writer property."
    gate_order_for_251: "Certification BEFORE the next full suite, deliberately. The certification mutates files in place; a gate reading a mutated tree is the defect measured on CARD-250, and gating a tree that may still change is waste. Nothing will read the tree while the reviewer mutates it."
    review_2_note: "Gate on 130823f running CONCURRENTLY with review 2 -- deliberate, and the review is told the host is loaded and to take no timing measurements. Wave 46 established that concurrency makes ms/test unreadable but does not change a verdict."
    mutation_check_owed: "Cycle 1 deferred(cost). Cycle 2 is told it must RUN if there are no gating findings, and must say so explicitly if it defers again -- the card cannot merge on a review whose certification was deferred."
    f001_decision: "Orchestrator call, recorded on the card: the page STAYS and says so when the board moved while the store was deciding -- not 'ask before leaving', which would put a question in front of an answer the owner has not read. Narrows AC-5's literal wording to the settled case; a one-line change if the owner wants the exit to win even then."
    f005_routed: "backlog @tech-debt -- four more surfaces draw the unversioned grid URL; the reviewer's ETag/Last-Modified option fixes all five at once AND keeps caching, which is what option 2 was rejected for. Touches app.py, so it wants its own slot."
    verified_by_orchestrator: "F-001, F-002 and F-003 all re-derived against the code before briefing the fix agent, including F-001's remedy premise (onBoardChange advances turn BEFORE its early return, so a turn captured at approve time is a true predicate). F-003 is a control that cannot fail: _approve_offered is read on /puzzles, where the locator matches nothing."
    reviewer_corrected_the_orchestrator: "On F-006 the brief claimed the inFlightIs comment cites the wrong guardrail. It does not -- the line is unchanged context and CARD-246's G-4 really is the accessibility rule. What remains is a bare G-N a reader of CARD-250's list resolves wrongly."
    implementation_commit: 318bf1d
    build_gate: "PASSED full 884s, EXIT=0 first attempt, 8390/1/0; collected 8391 vs main's 8364 = +27, all 27 in the card's own two test files"
    at: 2026-10-19T08:06:57Z
  queued: [CARD-251]
  conflict_edges:
    - pair: [CARD-250, CARD-251]
      reason: >-
        predicted: hotspot src/nonogram/admin/app.py, plus three more shared files
        (static/puzzle_edit.js, templates/puzzle_edit.html, templates/_puzzle_table.html)
        and the same browser-test file (tests/test_puzzle_editor_ui.py). overlap = 5/7 = 0.71
        against max_overlap 0.3. SERIALIZED: CARD-250 runs alone, CARD-251 starts through the
        slot refill once CARD-250 is MERGED, so its worktree branches from a main that already
        carries the renamed buttons.
  why_250_first: >-
    Not only the conflict edge. CARD-251 INHERITS CARD-250's rename: its confirmation dialog and
    its AC-9 warning are written against a bar whose exits read "Cancel" (leave) and "Back to edit"
    (stay). Run in the other order, CARD-251 would be written against labels that are about to
    change and every string assertion in it would need rewriting at the merge.
  effort_shape: "CARD-250 standard/1d/TDD false; CARD-251 architectural/1d/TDD true (its model is not downgraded, cmd-start-review step 0 spawn rule)."
  gate_condition: "DATABASE_URL SET (changed 2026-10-18, see gate_condition_change). Full-suite runs take the flock on meta/kanban/.full-suite.lock; implementation agents do not run the full suite."
  owner_decisions_carried_into_251: >-
    Both settled 2026-10-19 and recorded on the card: a regrade inside a published book is REFUSED
    (INV-009), a split two-up pair is ANNOUNCED and Approve stays available. G-3 forbids collapsing
    one into the other.

wave_47_complete:
  at: 2026-10-19T13:43:42Z
  cards: [CARD-250, CARD-251]
  merged: 2
  escalations: 1
  escalation: "CARD-250 cycle 3 escalated on a prose-only Important and the orchestrator OVERRIDE it, with the reversal path recorded on the card. Counted as an escalation rather than hidden, because the pipeline's answer was to involve the owner and the orchestrator decided otherwise."
  smoke_test: "SATISFIED by CARD-251's merge gate without a separate run: 8494 passed, 1 skipped, 0 failed, SOLO, and `git diff 73bbacd HEAD -- src tests migrations pyproject.toml meta/profiling` is empty, so main's code tree is byte-identical to the gated tree. CARD-251 branched from a main that already carried CARD-250, so that gate tested the union -- the same reasoning waves 43, 44 and 45 used."
  results:
    - id: CARD-250
      merge_commit: 71b5ec9
      review: "7.5 -> 8.0 -> 8.0 (3 cycles, 3 fix cycles)"
      actual: 0.4d
      estimate: 1d
    - id: CARD-251
      merge_commit: 49c5e82
      review: "8.0 -> 9.0 (2 cycles, 1 fix cycle), certification complete"
      actual: 0.4d
      estimate: 1d
  through_line: >-
    ONE defect family, found TEN times across two cards: a claim stated wider than its mechanism, or a
    control that cannot fail. Only FOUR came from review. The others came from the fix agents (three,
    one of them INSIDE the site it was sent to correct), from the AC/EC/G gate, from the merge gate, and
    from the orchestrator twice -- including twice in the orchestrator's OWN TOOLING (a gate guard that
    counted its own shell, then one that could only ever pass).
  criteria_amended: "FOUR: CARD-250's AC-5 and AC-7, CARD-251's AC-6 and AC-9. Criteria are being written narrower than the decisions they serve, and it is the MEASUREMENT rather than the review that keeps catching it. This belongs in the retrospective as a decompose-station finding, not as four separate card notes."
  orchestrator_defects: >-
    Recorded rather than smoothed over. (1) A full-suite gate run concurrently with a review carrying
    mutation_check: true, which read a mutated tree and produced 12 failures that were exactly that
    mutant's 12 kills. (2) A first diagnosis of that run -- CPU contention -- that was WRONG in a
    comfortable way; a retry would have come back green and buried the real hazard. (3) A gate guard
    that counted its own shell, reported 2 and 3 with nothing running, and led to a confident
    attribution that had to be withdrawn after it was committed. (4) Its replacement, which anchored on
    lowercase `python` and matched nothing on a machine whose interpreter is `Python` -- a guard that
    could only ever pass. (5) Four suite-timing timestamps written from `stat -t` local time and
    labelled Z. (6) A claim written into an amended AC that measurement refuted.
  what_the_full_suite_saw_that_reviews_could_not: >-
    CARD-251's merge gate went red on a frozen render-set registry the card's own test file
    invalidated. Two review cycles, a 16-mutant certification and an AC/EC/G-grade sweep all passed
    over it, because every one of them was told -- correctly -- not to run the full suite. The gates
    are not a formality after the reviews.
  owner_decisions_this_wave:
    - "the four buttons are fine, leave them (2026-10-19, after the CARD-250 render)"
    - "refuse the regrade inside a book (2026-10-19)"
    - "telling is fine, keep it as a warning (2026-10-19)"
  open_for_the_owner:
    - "CARD-250 render: the row's caption still says duck.png after the picture became a cat. Raised as a question, no answer, so it stays a question."
    - "CARD-251: a split pair the book's suffix ABSORBS is silent, because `changes` is a page-COUNT key. Faithful to the decision as worded; the owner may want the warning to fire on moved folios too."
    - "CARD-218 / CARD-219 are sized against a render-share figure that now includes a file whose two render mentions do not draw."

wave_48:
  dispatched: 2026-10-19T15:41:43Z
  concurrency: 2
  owner_instruction: "start the next wave (2026-10-19)"
  cards: [CARD-225, CARD-247]
  conflict_edges:
    - pair: [CARD-225, CARD-247]
      reason: >-
        NONE -- verified disjoint on every file, which is why these two genuinely run in parallel
        rather than sharing a wave and a clock. CARD-225 is export/layout.py, book_pdf_generator.py,
        book_plan.py, book_page_spec.py, book_manager.py, app.py and the book tests; CARD-247 is
        puzzle_solve.html, solver.js, admin.css and the solver tests. No shared path, no shared
        hotspot.
  why_these_two: >-
    CARD-225 is the board's only ready P1 and the one card that unblocks two others (CARD-226 and
    CARD-232, with CARD-233 behind CARD-232) -- it has been named the first refill candidate since
    wave 44. Every other print card collides with it on export/layout.py or book_pdf_generator.py,
    CARD-220 is tests/** (overlap 1.00, the wave-44 precedent), and CARD-249 and CARD-216 share the
    app.py hotspot. CARD-247 is the only eligible card disjoint from it.
  first_wave_under_the_new_curation: >-
    Both cards' System contracts were RE-DERIVED under meta/kanban/curation.md rather than inherited:
    CARD-225 keeps 17 of 60, CARD-247 keeps 16 of 57, and both now carry ADR-0039/R1-R3, which the
    old judgement had dropped on both wave-47 cards. CARD-247's previous contract was 8 rules chosen
    by hand and contained no ADR-0039.
  known_residue: >-
    The re-derivation also made the model's coarse scoping visible: a player-toolbar card now carries
    four book-geometry rules, because the ADRs' scopes resolve to all of CTX-001. Kept anyway -- the
    rule is mechanical and silent dropping is the thing the convention exists to stop. The fix is
    tightening the scope globs at the architect station, filed rather than improvised here.
  gate_discipline: >-
    Gates run SOLO under a guard that refuses a contended start and was verified both ways before use
    (1 with a live run, 0 idle). No gate runs while a review's mutation pass is live -- the CARD-250
    defect. Two cards in parallel means two build gates: they serialise on the full-suite lock.

card_252_started_mid_wave:
  at: 2026-10-19T17:33:41Z
  owner_instruction: "card the @ops engine fix for the next wave (2026-10-19), then: start CARD-252 now"
  concurrency_note: >-
    THIS TAKES THE BOARD TO THREE ACTIVE CARDS AGAINST THE OWNER'S STANDING CAP OF 2, on the owner's
    explicit instruction. Recorded rather than silently absorbed, because the cap exists for a reason
    and the reason still applies: it was set so merges run sequentially and never alongside concurrent
    full suites.
  conflict_edges:
    - pair: [CARD-252, CARD-225]
      reason: "NONE -- disjoint. CARD-252 is tests/conftest.py, src/nonogram/db/session.py and one new test file; CARD-225 is the book modules and the book tests."
    - pair: [CARD-252, CARD-247]
      reason: "NONE -- disjoint. CARD-247 is solver.js, admin.css, the two puzzle templates and the solver tests."
  scheduling_consequence: >-
    Three active cards make SOLO GATE WINDOWS scarcer, and every gate on this board must be solo --
    the guard refuses a contended start. CARD-225 and CARD-247 both still need merge gates, and
    CARD-252 will need a build gate. They serialise. Expect the wave to close on gate queueing rather
    than on agent time, and do not read any gate duration from this window as a cost trend.
  runtime_isolation: >-
    Checked rather than assumed: CARD-252 repeatedly DROPs and rebuilds a schema, which is exactly the
    hazard it exists to fix. It does so in its OWN derived test database (CARD-244), so it cannot
    disturb the siblings' reviews. The brief says so explicitly and names nonogram_poc as untouchable.

card_247_merged:
  at: 2026-10-19T18:42:52Z
  merge_commit: 8c807a6
  review: "9.0 -> 9.5 (2 cycles, 1 fix cycle + the orchestrator closing both cycle-2 minors)"
  certification: "complete -- 20 mutants, 0 unexpected survivors, all nine refusal branches re-derived rather than sampled, the 9x9 matrix recomputed from a SECOND hand-written implementation of the predicates"
  actual: 0.4d
  estimate: 0.5d
  gate: "8504 passed, 1 skipped, 0 failed in 958s, SOLO, first attempt; main verified byte-identical to the gated tree"
  two_defects_assertions_could_not_see: >-
    The first build drew three filled cells with collapsed borders, rendering as ONE BLACK BAR while
    every data-state assertion stayed green -- caught by looking at the render. The second was a
    control that could not fail, and it was the card's own: nine refusals asserted only as
    "problems is non-empty", so four could be deleted with the control still green.
  adr_0039_earned_its_place_within_hours: >-
    ADR-0039/R1 reached this card's contract only because the curation convention and the scope-glob
    fix landed earlier the same day; before that it had been dropped from CARD-250's and CARD-251's
    contracts by orchestrator judgement. The FIRST card to carry it caught a violation of it on cycle
    one. That is the clearest evidence this wave produced that the fix was the right one.
  owner_decision_applied: "rename the editor's button, keep Clear for the brush (2026-10-19). New name Reset, chosen because the player already calls the identical control that, so one change removed the collision and ended an inconsistency CARD-246 left. Pinned, because nothing pinned the label before."
  still_open_for_the_owner: "the PLAYER's confirmation still reads 'Clear the board?' / 'Clear board' under a button called Reset, two controls from the Clear brush. Raised, deliberately left alone, recorded as a decision rather than an oversight."

