resume_2026_10_21:
  at: 2026-10-21T22:07:30Z
  invoked: "run resume"
  mode: interactive
  found: >-
    Header read `wave: 48` with `active: [CARD-225, CARD-252]` -- four waves and six merged cards
    stale, last_updated 2026-10-19T07:15:55Z. It had been flagged to the owner twice without being
    edited, deliberately: clearing the dispatcher's own state is its command's job.
  candidates_diagnosed: >-
    Both candidates are `done` with merge commits on main (CARD-225 00b6ff0f, CARD-252 8cf7a54),
    their worktrees are gone and their branches are deleted. The recovery matrix in cmd-run step 3
    reads MISSING worktree -> "recreate worktree, restart implementation", which on a MERGED card
    would be destructive -- the matrix presumes status in_progress/review. Neither card is a recovery
    candidate; both were dropped from `active` and NOTHING was restarted.
  fallback_scan: >-
    Ran step 1's missing-file fallback as a cross-check even though the file existed. Across all 259
    cards: 240 done, 18 ready, 1 blocked (CARD-210, owner decision 2026-10-13), ZERO in_progress and
    ZERO review. No crashed agent, no orphaned worktree (`git worktree list` is main only), no board
    lock, no full-suite lock. There was nothing to recover -- the state was stale, not interrupted.
  escalated_untouched: "none -- `escalated: []`, and CARD-260's wave-51 escalation was resolved by owner-authorised rounds 4 and 5 before the merge."
  stale_keys_neutralised: >-
    Four top-level keys were WAVE-44 leftovers sitting under a later header and have been renamed
    with a `wave_44_` prefix: merged_this_wave, refill_deferred, refill_deferred_resolved, refilled
    and conflict_edges. `conflict_edges` was the hazard, not the untidiness: cmd-run step 6 tells a
    resume to "take conflict_edges from dispatcher.state if the file survived", and every edge in
    that set is predicted against CARD-222, merged in wave 44 -- this resume would have adopted a
    seven-wave-old edge set as current. Renamed rather than deleted; the history is the record.
  position_now: >-
    Wave 51 complete and verified. Wave 52 is the current wave with ONE card, CARD-259 (P2,
    tech-debt, trivial, 0.5d, TDD true, skill python-pro), status `ready`, both dependencies merged --
    CARD-257 b441ba00 created the single source it points at, CARD-260 62f52ec6 committed the
    instrument it is told to CONSUME rather than rebuild. No card is dispatched; `active: []`.
  not_dispatched_here: >-
    `run resume` step 6 says to continue as a normal run into the next wave, and mode is
    `interactive`, which asks at the wave boundary. The recovery itself needed no decision and was
    done; the wave-52 dispatch is a decision and is the owner's, consistent with this board's
    practice of recording owner_decision at every wave boundary (see wave_45, wave_48).

wave_52:
  dispatched: 2026-10-21T22:18:12Z
  concurrency: 2
  owner_instruction: "run resume (2026-10-21), then at the wave boundary: fill both slots -- CARD-206 + CARD-259"
  cards: [CARD-206, CARD-259]
  conflict_edges:
    - pair: [CARD-206, CARD-259]
      reason: >-
        NONE -- intersection of their Touches sets is EMPTY, overlap 0.00 against max_overlap 0.30. Both
        cards do touch a declared hotspot, but NOT THE SAME ONE, which is what the rule turns on: CARD-206
        touches src/nonogram/admin/book_pdf_generator.py, CARD-259 touches src/nonogram/db/** via
        src/nonogram/db/session.py. Different hotspots do not serialize. Checked rather than assumed,
        because a glance at the hotspot list makes this pair look like it should serialize.
  runtime_edge_checked: >-
    CARD-259 touches tests/conftest.py and repeatedly exercises the engine-reset path, which is the shape
    that cost wave 44 a fix attempt when two cards shared nonogram_test. It cannot recur here: CARD-244
    gave each worktree its own derived database, and the two worktrees are separate checkouts, so a
    conftest edit in one is invisible to the other. nonogram_poc is named untouchable in CARD-259's G-3.
  owner_decision: >-
    Asked at the wave boundary (mode: interactive) with four options. The owner chose to fill both slots
    rather than run CARD-259 alone, which was the alternative the wave plan prescribed. Recorded because
    the reason for asking is informative: waves 49-52 are SEVEN consecutive cards of ops/tech-debt/enabler
    work, every one of them sourced from the previous card's own review or gate, and CARD-259 is the FOURTH
    GENERATION of CARD-252 (252 -> 254 -> 257 -> 259). The last `feature` card merged was in wave 48 on
    2026-10-19, while 11 interior-redesign cards have been unblocked since CARD-222 and none had been
    dispatched. CARD-206 is the first of those. The owner's choice runs the product chain and the
    tech-debt chain at once instead of choosing between them.
  cross_card_family_blindness: >-
    Worth naming as a gap in the machinery, not just as a wave observation: `review.family_regression: on`
    detects a loop feeding on itself across the CYCLES OF ONE CARD. Nothing on this board detects the same
    shape across CARDS. The 252 -> 254 -> 257 -> 259 chain is exactly that shape and it passed four
    dispatches unflagged.
  contracts_re_derived_at_dispatch: >-
    Both cards' System contracts were derived here under meta/kanban/curation.md rather than inherited:
    CARD-206 keeps 14 of 42, CARD-259 keeps 10 of 29. CARD-206's inherited contract was assembled
    2026-10-17 under the superseded "ship 6-14 by judgement" convention and carried NO ADR-0039 -- the
    third instance of the exact failure that convention was replaced for (CARD-250, CARD-251, and nearly
    CARD-254/255). CARD-259 had no contract section at all, and its own notes asked for this derivation at
    dispatch, naming the case-insensitive read. Both now satisfy curation.md's sanity check. The kept
    counts match the two comparable cards of wave 48/49 exactly -- CARD-225 (book layout) kept 14, CARD-254
    (a guard/prose card) kept 10 -- which is a consistency signal, not a coincidence.
  two_card_defects_found_at_dispatch:
    - card: CARD-259
      what: "AC-4 pinned a STALE hash -- `bdb10905...` (dump length 9154)."
      detail: >-
        The value is correct for b441ba00, where the card was cut, and the card's own stated method
        reproduces it there exactly -- which is what establishes the method and condemns the number.
        CARD-258 then changed session.py's code, so at 4584caf4 and at main the docstring-stripped AST
        dump is 9487 bytes hashing to `49978874...`. Asserted against main as written, AC-4 would have red
        on the card's FIRST commit for a reason with nothing to do with the card. AC-4 now says to derive
        the expected value from `git merge-base HEAD main` instead of trusting a literal. This is the
        wave-48 figure rule in its purest form -- no mutant catches a stale number.
    - card: CARD-259
      what: "Cut 0.5d / `trivial` against the owner's own wave-50 estimation rule."
      detail: >-
        The rule is that a card whose subject is a claim or a measurement floors at 0.75d and may not be
        `trivial`, whatever its diff size. This card's subject is a claim. Corrected to 0.75d / standard.
        Evidence is now two waves deep: wave 50's two cards were cut at 0.25d and took ~0.8d each; wave
        51's CARD-260 was cut at 0.75d and took 3.3d. 0.75d is the floor, not a prediction.
  baseline_verified_before_dispatch: >-
    60 passed, 2 skipped, EXIT=0 on tests/test_db_engine_lifecycle.py + tests/test_book_toc.py at
    27ca88c7, with the lock-path environment switch pointed at a scratch path (announced) so the board's
    own lock was neither created nor read. Run so that a red in either card's first cycle is attributable
    to the card and not inherited. The switch is deliberately NOT named here: this file is not one of
    CARD-255's two exempt prefixes and `.state` is not one of its prose shapes, so naming it reds the
    tracked-tree scan in tests/test_full_suite_lock.py. That happened once already, from backlog.md
    during CARD-260's merge (commit e821f579), and the remedy then was the same as now -- reword the
    prose, never widen the guard. Cards and review reports are exempt surfaces; the dispatcher's own
    state file is not, and that distinction is correct.
  config_deviation_found_and_not_silently_continued:
    what: "review.test_scope in config.yml is `full`, and has been since wave 26 (its only commit)."
    consequence: >-
      CARD-260's review cycles ran IMPACT-SCOPED gates (e.g. "161 passed / 3 skipped"), which is not what
      this project's config asks for. The narrowing may well be the right call -- the config's own comment
      says to flip to `impact` "if the suite becomes the bottleneck", the backlog carries an item arguing
      the merge gate should be the only full run, and the full suite is ~16.5 min which two parallel cards
      must serialize on the lock. But the deviation was not recorded at the time and the config was not
      changed, so the card's gate evidence and the project's declared gate policy disagree. Flagged for an
      owner decision before wave 52's first build gate; the gates themselves are hours away, so nothing is
      blocked by leaving it open now.
  gate_discipline: >-
    Unchanged from wave 48: gates run SOLO under a guard that refuses a contended start, two cards mean
    two build gates and they serialise on meta/kanban/.full-suite.lock, and no gate runs while a review's
    mutation pass is live (the CARD-250 defect). Do not read any gate duration from this window as a cost
    trend -- two cards share this machine.

wave_52_phases:
  CARD-206:
    phase: implementation
    review_cycle: 0
  CARD-259:
    phase: review
    review_cycle: 1
    implementation_commit: d85b48b0
    build_gate: "impact-scoped, 8 files: 815 passed, 2 skipped, 0 failed, EXIT=0"
    build_gate_scope_deviation: >-
      config.yml says review.test_scope: full; this gate ran IMPACT-scoped. The reason is structural, not
      a preference: a full-suite gate takes the board lock, and CARD-255's guard raises pytest.UsageError
      on a foreign holder, so a full run here would have red every test run CARD-206's agent made in its
      own worktree for ~16.5 minutes. `max_concurrent: 2` and `test_scope: full` cannot both be honoured
      with two cards live -- the first card to reach a gate breaks the other. cmd-start-review resolves it
      the same way (impact runs take no lock; the full suite runs at the merge gate, one of the two
      backstops that make narrowing safe). CARD-260's cycles did this and recorded nothing. Owner decision
      owed: flip test_scope to `impact` and make the practice the policy, or lower max_concurrent to 1 so
      `full` is actually reachable.
    findings_filed_about_another_card: 3

wave_52_complete:
  at: 2026-10-22T07:37:15Z
  cards: [CARD-259, CARD-206]
  merged: 2
  escalations: 2
  merges:
    - id: CARD-259
      merge_commit: 5f18e2f5
      review_score: "8.5 (cycle 3/3)"
      actual: 0.35d
      estimate: 0.75d
      accuracy: 0.46x
      gate: >-
        8785 passed, 1 skipped, 0 failed, EXIT=0, DATABASE_URL SET. Its FIRST run was RED on the
        orchestrator's own impact-set harness -- a hard-coded .venv interpreter, where a card worktree has
        no .venv and a merge gate always runs from one. Fixed on main (1c370193) with sys.executable plus
        a control over the whole class, then re-gated.
    - id: CARD-206
      merge_commit: 3addf01b
      review_score: "8.5 (cycle 3/3)"
      actual: 0.39d
      estimate: 0.5d
      accuracy: 0.78x
      gate: "8810 passed, 1 skipped, 0 failed, EXIT=0, DATABASE_URL SET -- the first full suite with both cards and the impact-set harness in one tree"
  escalations_detail:
    - id: CARD-206
      trigger: "family_regression, streak 2 -- GENUINE"
      family: "the page-position guard keeps claiming a reach it has not measured"
      resolution: "owner chose SLICE; the guard left as CARD-262 and the card merged its title page with the claim narrowed"
    - id: CARD-259
      trigger: "family_regression asserted at streak 1 -- the orchestrator's error"
      resolution: >-
        The detector needs streak >= 2 and it was 1 (cycle 1's findings were the implementation's, so a
        regression was impossible). The escalation was taken one cycle early. The owner's chosen route
        was `continue` plus a deletions-only constraint, which is one of the procedure's own four routes,
        and the card then converged. Corrected on the card; cost was one owner decision the procedure did
        not require.
  gate_condition_restored: >-
    Both merge gates ran with DATABASE_URL SET against per-worktree derived databases, each name guarded
    twice before any server was touched (must end in `_test`, must not contain `nonogram_poc`). 1 skip
    each, not 27. This RETIRES the declared bound CARD-260 merged under, where the condition was unset
    and the gate exercised ~26 fewer tests than the one that certified CARD-258.
  the_waves_lesson: >-
    Both cards produced the same shape from opposite directions and it is worth promoting at the
    retrospective: CARD-259's mechanism outran its claims, CARD-206's claims outran its mechanism. The
    board's own ADR-0039/R1 covers both, but what actually caught them was neither review nor the author
    -- it was the AC/EC/G gate run BLIND of the review's verdicts, which failed both cards after they had
    passed review (CARD-259's AC-2 on wording the orchestrator wrote; CARD-206's G-3 on a test name that
    collects nothing, pytest exit 5, the identical mistake already recorded at CARD-221.md:227).
  estimate_turn: >-
    Two cards in a row came in UNDER (0.46x, 0.78x) after five waves of 3x-4x overruns. The owner's
    wave-50 claim-card floor forced CARD-259 off trivial/0.5d at dispatch and the work then cost less
    than the floor -- the first evidence the floor is a floor rather than a new bias in the other
    direction.
  queue_note: >-
    CARD-206's real diff was 28 files against 7 declared, and it overlaps the Touches of 12 ready cards,
    two of them at 100% and 89% (CARD-219, CARD-218). Those two touch no declared hotspot of their own,
    so the predictor would score them parallel-safe against a card like this. tests/helpers/book_corpus.py
    was added to conflict.hotspots and CARD-206's own Touches corrected, which covers the pairs that
    DECLARE it -- the residue is in backlog.md as an @ops item. All twelve now need a rebase.

resume_2026_10_22:
  at: 2026-10-22T07:52:00Z
  invoked: "run resume"
  mode: interactive
  found: "nothing to recover -- `active: []`, `escalated: []`, and wave 52 closed cleanly minutes earlier."
  fallback_scan: >-
    Ran step 1's missing-file fallback as a cross-check even though the state was current. Across all 261
    cards: 242 done, 18 ready, 1 blocked (CARD-210, owner decision 2026-10-13), **zero in_progress and
    zero review**. No worktree but the main checkout, no board lock, no escalation outstanding.
  position_now: "wave 52 complete; wave 53 is the next wave and nothing is dispatched."
  dispatchable: 14
  held_by_dependencies:
    - "CARD-218, CARD-219 -> CARD-207, CARD-208, CARD-209"
    - "CARD-230 -> CARD-229"
    - "CARD-233 -> CARD-232"
  the_queue_is_structurally_SERIAL_and_this_is_the_finding: >-
    **9 of the 14 dispatchable cards touch `src/nonogram/admin/book_pdf_generator.py`** -- CARD-207, 208,
    209, 226, 227, 229, 231, 232 and 262 -- which is a declared hotspot, so they mutually serialize and
    **at most ONE can be in flight at a time**. 44 conflict edges among the 14; only 47 of the 91 possible
    pairs can run concurrently, and every one of those pairs is "one book card plus one non-book card".
    The non-book five are CARD-215, 216, 220, 249 and 261. Secondary clusters: `export/layout.py` binds
    six (208, 209, 215, 226, 227, 232), `app.py` binds three (216, 229, 249), and
    `tests/helpers/book_corpus.py` -- added to the hotspot list this session -- binds six (209, 226, 227,
    229, 231, 232).
    **Consequence for the owner's concurrency cap of 2: it buys nothing on this queue unless one slot is
    deliberately spent on a non-book card.** Two book cards can never run together. That is a property of
    the print chain, not of the predictor, and it is the honest reason waves 44-48 kept serializing.
  stale_premise_warning: >-
    CARD-218 and CARD-219 are held by CARD-207/208/209, and both were cut BEFORE CARD-206 rewrote the
    interior page map. CARD-219's `Touches` is 8 files, **all 8 edited by CARD-206**; CARD-218's is 8 of
    9. Their bodies cite page numbers and counts that moved by one. They should be re-read against main
    before dispatch, not merely rebased -- the same class as CARD-259's AC-4, which shipped a hash pinned
    at a revision that had already moved.
  not_dispatched_here: >-
    `run resume` step 6 continues into the next wave, and mode is `interactive`, which asks at the
    boundary. The recovery needed no decision and there was none to make; the wave-53 dispatch is a
    decision and is the owner's, consistent with this board's practice of recording an owner_decision at
    every wave boundary (wave_45, wave_48, wave_52).

wave_54:
  dispatched: 2026-10-22
  cards: [CARD-264]
  concurrency_note: >
    Three worktrees are live at once (CARD-261 fix 2, CARD-262 fix 1, CARD-264 implementation),
    which is above the usual two but below dispatcher.max_concurrent. It is safe only because no
    full-suite run is permitted in any of them: every agent redirects the lock to a scratch path
    and scopes its runs, so CARD-255's guard cannot red a sibling.
  base: >
    CARD-264's worktree is cut from feb3e6b5 -- CARD-261's fix-1 commit -- not from main. CARD-261
    rewrites CLAUDE.md's layout block and moves every line below it, so a main-based worktree would
    have had the implementation write controls against a file about to change underneath it in the
    same two paths. A rebase onto main is owed after CARD-261 merges, before CARD-264's gate.
  conflict_edges: >
    CARD-264 shares CLAUDE.md and tests/test_cli.py with CARD-261 -- 2 of 5 paths, which is 0.4
    against dispatcher.max_overlap 0.3 and would normally serialize. Dispatched concurrently anyway
    because the two cards collide in NEITHER path by construction: G-4 on CARD-261 and G-1 on
    CARD-264 were written as mirror images so every line of CLAUDE.md has exactly one owner, and
    CARD-261's cycle 2 verified the out-of-block defects present and byte-identical from the other
    side. This is a deliberate override of the overlap score on measured evidence, not a miss.
  contract: projection 10, kept 5, dropped 5 (ADR-0038/R7-R8 review-lens; ADR-0039/R1-R3 reach-limited)

card_261_done:
  merge_commit: ac63e17a
  tested_tree: ff4374f6
  at: 2026-10-22
  gates: >-
    Run in BOTH configurations, sequentially, which is a first on this board: the project
    default (no database variable) gave 8823 passed / 27 skipped / 0 failed, and the
    DB-mode run against a freshly migrated nonogram_test gave 8849 passed / 1 skipped / 0
    failed. The two reconcile to the test -- 21 tests pass only with a database configured,
    and 21 plus the 5 that needed the migration equals the 26-skip difference -- so nothing
    moved between the runs except the database being present. That reconciliation is also
    the first measurement of how much the project-default gate cannot see: twenty-six tests.
  concurrency: >-
    The owner's cap of 2 is restored by this merge; two cards remain active. The merge ran
    with no other full suite on the machine, and the DB-mode gate refuses to start if it
    finds one, so the cap's stated purpose is enforced by the script rather than remembered.

